# Stack Trace Explanation

The error occurs because `order` is `undefined` when the component first renders, so accessing `order.items.map()` fails.

This happens during the initial render before your API call completes and sets the `order` data.

# Fix

Add a **guard clause** to handle the undefined state:

```tsx
function OrderSummary({ order }) {
  if (!order) {
    return <div>Loading order...</div>;
  }
  return <ul>{order.items.map(i => <li key={i.sku}>{i.name}</li>)}</ul>;
}
```

## Alternative Approaches

**Optional chaining** (more concise):
```tsx
return <ul>{order?.items?.map(i => <li key={i.sku}>{i.name}</li>)}</ul>;
```

**With nullish coalescing** (provides fallback):
```tsx
return <ul>{(order?.items ?? []).map(i => <li key={i.sku}>{i.name}</li>)}</ul>;
```

**With default props**:
```tsx
function OrderSummary({ order = { items: [] } }) {
  return <ul>{order.items.map(i => <li key={i.sku}>{i.name}</li>)}</ul>;
}
```

**Best Practice**: Use the first approach with an explicit loading state since the user is redirected right after checkout—they'll appreciate feedback that their order is being processed.

